    20	A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that under uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. Two registered tests, the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, compare it at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and a verified-history comparator, C$^*$.
