   243	\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase's effect given newest-first targeting is larger (\site{r_q2_fixed_pg1} under D2+H3 and \site{r_q2_fixed_pg1_x} under X7-SC, averaged over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting's effect without the priority phase (above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
