   369	\textbf{Original claim} & \textbf{FAIL} & \\\midrule
   370	Amended, D2+H3 absolute: gross efficiency & PASS & lower \evone{0.9980} \\
   371	Amended, D2+H3 absolute: gain & PASS & upper \evone{0.0719} \\
   372	Amended, gain reduction vs B2 & PASS & lower \evone{0.487} \\
   373	Amended, gain reduction vs B3 & PASS & lower \evone{0.276} \\
   374	\textbf{Amended claim} & \textbf{PASS} & \\\bottomrule
   375	\end{tabular}
   376	\caption{Registered verdict of the first test (unchanged), on \evone{40} fresh seeds at $\beta = \evone{0.05}$ and the median position. Bounds are one-sided: \evone{95}\% for the rule clause and the absolute part, \evone{98.75}\% for the comparisons. The amended pass also requires the H2 rule clause and admission of the comparison bounds, whose interval procedures were admitted on development distributions with B3 as proxy; nominal coverage for the selected R3 is not established.}\label{tab:verdict1}
   377	\end{table}
   378	
   379	\paragraph{Result.} The original claim failed against H2: R3 passed the rule clause, but neither audited baseline met either baseline criterion (Table~\ref{tab:verdict1}). The amended claim passed: under D2+H3 the mean gains were \evone{0.570} for B2, \evone{0.353} for B3, and \evone{0.071} for R3. These are normalised excess focal profits, not losses of honest surplus; and the first report's sentence that R3 matched B3 and B2 under H2 ``within noise'' was unsupported, since no equivalence test was run (published erratum).
   380	
   381	\paragraph{The language-model attacker.} H1, a language model playing the focal provider at $T = \evone{100}$, was exploratory. Its R3 result is inconclusive by the registered rule, because R3's audit selection cannot be expressed in the description vocabulary given to the actor; Amendment 4 raised H1's cost cap once after 11 of its 12 cells had been seen.
   382	
   383	\paragraph{Failure modes.} Exploratory robustness cells on reused development seeds found three: at high audit cost ($\rho = \evone{1}$) R3's terminal reserve consumes the whole allowance and R3 audits nothing early; high noise lowers every audited rule's efficiency; and coalitions of entrants overload the newcomer priority. The full timeline and the robustness table are in the supplement; the record and its claims are unchanged.
   384	
   385	\section{Registration detail}\label{sec:app-design}
   386	\paragraph{Development.} All choices before the draw were development, on the headline market and the development instance dev0 (branch \texttt{two20k}): a benchmark priced every cell; the comparator was selected and the tuned variants were tuned; the defenders were frozen at a signed commit; a fresh session of another model (gpt-6.1-sol), started by the author, wrote H4 against the frozen defenders; after a machine restart the executor resumed the same session at the author's instruction, without reading its log or bundle, committed the bundle in the author's separate clone, and only its hash entered the protocol; a coverage check on frozen development attacks admitted calibration multipliers $\kappa = \const{0.5}$ for Q1 and $\kappa = \const{0.25}$ for Q4; and the predictions were written and hashed. Budget caps were \const{12} USD for development and \const{40} USD for evaluation, within a written authorisation of \const{52} USD. The evaluation cap forced three registered cuts: the tuned variants, H4, and the D2 extras run on the headline market only.
   387	
   388	\paragraph{Deviations from the protocol.} Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered instance file holds the calibration computed on the author's arm64 machine. In each fresh market, between \const{1} and \const{6} provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in the last place, a relative difference near $10^{\const{-16}}$); the headline market is unaffected. The runner's verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run's configuration hash, every differing value is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the configurations match, not that the decisions are unchanged, since small input differences can change discrete simulation branches; the re-evaluation below holds the selected attacks and references fixed and does not repeat search or reference selection under the registered configurations. We therefore re-evaluated the frozen policies of every fresh-market cell that Q1 and Q4 use on the registration platform with the registered configurations, against the frozen references: per-seed gains change in every cell, cell means move by less than one hundredth, and every Q1 and Q4 decision is unchanged (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/stability.py}; Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability}). The report's addendum lists the hashes, states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell's references, which qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from \const{40} to \const{43} USD and the written authorisation from \const{52} to \const{55} USD so that every registered cell could run; no analysis changed. The exploratory follow-ups after the report ran under a separate follow-up cap of \const{72} USD (program limit \const{123} USD, the author's approval of 12 November 2026). Recorded spend: identity-replacement follow-up \const{1.65} USD; churn at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, \const{4.99} USD; churn at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03}, at most \const{7.70} USD; runner-up comparator, at most \const{3.14} USD; continued searches, \const{2.76} USD for a first attempt that lost its results to a killed worker and \const{3.40} USD for the rerun (the two upper bounds are ceiling estimates; the others are billed amounts or balance differences).
   389	
   390	
   391	\section{Parameters and definitions}\label{sec:app-params}\label{app:params}
   392	First test and headline market: providers $i = 1, \dots, 10$ have $q^{\max}_i = (0.60, 0.65, \dots, 1.00, 1.00)$, $b_i = (0.40, 0.38, \dots, 0.22)$, $a_i = 0.02$, $k_i = 6$, $[v_{\min}, v_{\max}] = [0.6, 1.4]$, $\sigma = 0.05$, $\beta = 0.05$, $\rho = 0.2$. Fresh markets of the second test: $N$, $J$, $T$, $\sigma$, $\beta$, $\rho$, and $v_{\min}$ as at the headline; $q^{\max}$ ten sorted draws from $U[0.55, 1.0]$; $b_i$ falling linearly in the quality rank between $b_{\mathrm{hi}} \sim U[0.34, 0.46]$ and $b_{\mathrm{lo}} \sim U[0.18, 0.26]$ with noise $U[-0.02, 0.02]$; $a_i \sim U[0, 0.05]$; $k_i$ uniform in $\{4, \dots, 8\}$; $v_{\max} \sim U[1.2, 1.6]$. The focal provider is at the median position. The first-best surplus $S^*$ of a seed is the sum over rounds of the maximum-weight assignment of jobs to capacity slots at full effort.
   393	
   394	\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
   395	\adjustbox{max width=\linewidth}{\input{tables/comparator_dev.tex}}
   396	\caption{Development comparator selection (before the lock; development records): each credibility configuration's selected D2+H3 attack gain and gross efficiency at the inner search budget, and the two configurations rechecked at full budget, at the headline market and dev0, with R3's pacing and the fixed pair. Score: the development objective, the minimum over markets and families of the smaller of an efficiency margin and a gain margin, with the market and family that decide it; the efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration.}\label{tab:comparator-dev}
   397	\end{table}
   398	
   399	\section{Proofs}\label{sec:app-proofs}\label{app:proofs}
   400	\paragraph{Lemma~\ref{lem:local}.} Shading saves $sg$. Each audited escrow is refunded with probability $d$ instead of $d_0$. Given $K$, independence of delivery noise gives the probabilities $1 - (1-d)^K$ and $1 - (1-d_0)^K$ of at least one failure; the continuation loss is incurred once. Taking expectations over $U$ gives the identity.
   401	
   402	\paragraph{Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}.} Let $V_{\mathrm{clean}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{fail}}$ be the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one, so $C_U = V_{\mathrm{clean}} - V_{\mathrm{fail}}$. By the first condition, immediate free replacement attains the clean distribution of future states after a failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \ge V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. By the second, any plan after a failure (keeping the banned identity and waiting, or replacing at any later time) can be copied after no failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \le V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. Hence $C_U = 0$. For B2 and B3 the second condition holds because a failed identity carries only its ban, with no priority or payment privilege, a clean provider can copy waiting by not offering and can itself replace its identity, no bond is lost, and the audit budget and rival states do not depend on the focal identity's history; replacement restores eligibility before the next allocation. Refunds of the failed jobs in the current round still enter Lemma~\ref{lem:local}. A rule that gave priority to an identity that waits out its ban would violate the second condition, and the corollary would not apply to it.
   403	
   404	\paragraph{Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}.} R3's priority list is ordered by age group, newest first. Within a group, the audit keys are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables, so the group's jobs are exchangeable and the selected subset is uniform among them; delivery noise is independent of selection.
   405	
   406	\paragraph{Theorem~\ref{thm:renewal}.} The spell length is geometric with mean $1/q_E$; since survival to round $t$ is determined by earlier rounds, the expected spell profit is $r_E/q_E$. Adding the recovery's rewards and entry charges and dividing by the expected cycle length gives $\bar\Pi$ by the renewal-reward theorem~\cite{vlasiou2014renewal}. A cycle's surplus is at most $(r_E - h)/q_E + \epsilon\,\mathbb{E}D$; dividing by $1/q_E + \mathbb{E}D$ and using $\mathbb{E}D \ge L$ gives the bound.
   407	
   408	\paragraph{Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal}.} With $C_U = 0$, $A'(0) = s b - \phi(\tau/\sigma)\,\sigma^{-1}\,\mathbb{E}\sum_{j \in U} M_j \ge s\,(b - v_{\max}\phi(\tau/\sigma)/\sigma)$, since $M_j \le v_{\max}$ and $K \le s$.
   409	
   410	\bibliographystyle{plainurl}
   411	\bibliography{refs}
   412	\end{document}

