   222	\section{What the results show and what they do not}\label{sec:discussion}
   223	% BEGIN-EV2
   224	\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section~\ref{sec:results}, which holds the parameters and search budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
   225	
   226	\paragraph{What they do not support.} The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empirical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished, and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C$^*$ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.
   227	% END-EV2
   228	\paragraph{Exact but costly checks.} The model's audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost charged to the operator's budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object, that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model's audit cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their claims.
   229	
   230	\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn it falls and reverses (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the lowest rate. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
   231	
   232	\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
   233	% BEGIN-EV2
   234	For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and its measured advantage reverses: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5}. Against verified-history targeting, age-ranked audit priority holds its own in this model only while the attacker is almost the only newcomer; against uniform audits it survives moderate churn but not the highest rate tested. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
   235	% END-EV2
   236	
   237	\paragraph{Availability.} The paper artifact will have its own release commit and archive hash. It identifies the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag \texttt{protocol-v2} and preserves the original tagged-code report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed, and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the deviation evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, and the paper sources. One command (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/reproduce\_deviation.py}) checks the artifact against the tag and reproduces the second test's report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper's second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability output, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in \texttt{docs/revision/paper-v2}, \texttt{docs/revision/posthoc}, and \texttt{docs/revision/audit}, and the first test's records and verdict are in the release as recorded in October~\const{2026}.
