   306	\paragraph{Predictions.} Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} score every registered prediction from the report. For Q4-b, the original scoring script compared the report's raw component $p$-values, although the prediction is registered on calibrated values; the corrected script compares the calibrated values, \site{r_pred_q4b_g} against \site{r_pred_q4b_e} on each of fresh0, fresh2, and fresh5. The prediction remains fulfilled and the total is unchanged: a scoring correction with no change of verdict, separate from the corrected labels of Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4}. The original numerical scoring path, preserved in the released scoring output, retains estimates from completed searches even when their doubling checks were marked incomplete; it counts \site{r_pred_n_ful} predictions fulfilled and \site{r_pred_n_fail} failed. A separate eligibility analysis applies the two filters below. The registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design's search-quality checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate for the prescribed search procedure; the rule does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} adds two post hoc eligibility readings. Starting from the listed inputs of each scored comparison, we propagate flags through recorded upstream links, including references, attack banks, and warm starts. An inherited flag identifies a potentially relevant ancestor; it does not establish that the flagged candidate was selected or that the statistic would change. The map is released with the scorer, and neither sensitivity is designated as the official retrospective score. If a flagged listed input or recorded ancestor makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: \site{r_pred_q_ful} predictions are fulfilled, \site{r_pred_q_fail} fail, and