   188	\section{Honest identity churn}\label{sec:churn}
   189	% BEGIN-EV2
   190	In the registered markets every honest provider keeps one identity for the whole episode, so after the start the newest identities are mostly the attacker's. This section asks what remains of R3's advantage when honest providers also replace identities. It is an exploratory follow-up after the report: it changes no registered decision, and its intervals hold the six markets and the selected policies fixed.
   191	
   192	\paragraph{Design.} Each of the nine honest opponents retires its identity and re-enters under a fresh one with probability $p$ in each round, at $p = \const{0.01}$, \const{0.02}, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05} (mean identity lifetimes of \const{100}, \const{50}, about \const{33}, and \const{20} rounds; \site{ch_reps1}, \site{ch_reps2}, \site{ch_reps3}, and \site{ch_reps5} honest replacements per episode). For B3, R3, and C$^*$ on the six fresh markets at $\rho=\const{0.2}$ we reran the registered D2+H3 search, evaluation, and doubling check, each against the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn; the reference menus were not regenerated. The rates are illustrative, not calibrated to any market. The plan and four expectations for $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} were written before any churn search, after a timing check on one market had shown the old attack's profit under R3 rising with $p$; the rates \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} were added by amendment after those results were seen, with four further expectations written before any run at these rates. Churn keeps capacity and quality fixed, but it also resets an honest provider's history and lets it leave a false ban, so it does not isolate age information.
   193	
   194	\paragraph{The advantage falls with the churn rate.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn} and Figure~\ref{fig:churn} give the pooled gains and contrasts. The gain against R3 rises from \site{ch_r3_0} without churn to \site{ch_r3_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$ ([\site{ch_r3_5_lo}, \site{ch_r3_5_hi}]), while the gains against C$^*$ and B3 change much less (from \site{ch_c_0} to \site{ch_c_5}, and from \site{ch_b_0} to \site{ch_b_5}). The C$^*$ minus R3 difference falls from \site{ch_d_0} to \site{ch_d_1}, \site{ch_d_2}, \site{ch_d_3}, and \site{ch_d_5} as $p$ rises; the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_0} without churn and \site{ch_q_1}, \site{ch_q_2}, \site{ch_q_3}, and \site{ch_q_5} at the four rates. The pooled comparison with C$^*$ thus reverses between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, and the comparison with uniform audits between \const{0.03} and \const{0.05}: at $p = \const{0.05}$ the attack earns more against R3 than against the rule without audit priority. At $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is more than three times the materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$, in C$^*$'s favour.
   195	
   196	\paragraph{How firm the reversals are.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on \site{ch_q_5_neg} with the frozen searches, and on \site{ch_d_5_dneg} and \site{ch_q_5_dneg} with the doubled searches (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{ch_d_5_d}; B3 minus R3: \site{ch_q_5_d}). Exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{ch_t_d5_lo}, \site{ch_t_d5_hi}] for C$^*$ minus R3 and [\site{ch_t_q5_lo}, \site{ch_t_q5_hi}] for B3 minus R3; they reach zero because the market values are very unequal, not because the sign varies. The frozen B3 value at this rate depends on one flagged cell (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn}, doubled row). At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_3_neg} of six markets, but at $p = \const{0.02}$ only on \site{ch_d_2_neg}: the pooled reversal at that rate comes mostly from one market. At $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1_x} with the frozen searches and \site{ch_d_1_d} with the doubled ones, so the comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved there, while the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_1_x} and \site{ch_q_1_d}. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} and \site{ch_inc_mid} of the \site{ch_inc_mid_n} at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is \site{ch_flag_max}. Reselecting each rule's reference from its frozen menu under churn changes the reference in \site{ch_resel} of the \site{ch_resel_n} cells, by at most \site{ch_resel_max} in gain.
   197	
   198	\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it stays near a third (\site{ch_fa_c5} at $p = \const{0.05}$) and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. On two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. The decline is gradual, not a threshold, and it does not explain both crossings: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while B3 minus R3 stays positive until between \const{0.03} and \const{0.05}, because the gain also depends on the rest of the selected attack. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
   199	
   200	\paragraph{Efficiency.} Under its selected attack, R3's gross efficiency falls from \site{ch_eff_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_eff_5} at \const{0.05}; C$^*$'s stays at \site{ch_eff_c5} and B3's is \site{ch_eff_b5}. The registered efficiency condition is a no-churn result.
   201	
   202	\paragraph{Expectations.} Scored on point estimates under both search versions, all eight expectations are met: $g_{\const{0.05}}(\mathrm{R3})$ above its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e1}''); C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.05} below its no-churn value (``\site{ch_e2}''); B3 minus R3 positive on all six markets at \const{0.01} (``\site{ch_e3}''); the audited share of honest replacements in their first ten rounds higher under R3 than under C$^*$ and B3 at \const{0.05}, \site{ch_sh_r3} against \site{ch_sh_c} and \site{ch_sh_b} (``\site{ch_e4}''); that share under R3 falling across the four rates (``\site{ch_e5}''); C$^*$ minus R3 lower at \const{0.03} than at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e6}''); $g_{\const{0.02}}(\mathrm{R3})$ below the midpoint of the no-churn and \const{0.05} values (``\site{ch_e7}''); and B3 minus R3 positive on at least five of six markets at \const{0.02} (``\site{ch_e8}''). The expectations were directional and carried little risk once the timing check had been seen.
   203	
   204	\paragraph{Scope.} Only one verified-history comparator, C$^*$, was run under churn; the runner-up comparator of Section~\ref{sec:results} was evaluated without churn only, so this section does not show that verified-history targeting in general is robust to churn. Within this model, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ exists only while the attacker is almost the only source of new identities, and its advantage over uniform audits survives moderate churn but not the highest rate tested.
   205	
