H4's code was committed only by its hash before the lock and released after the report review; it is in \texttt{sealed2/h4/} of the release (bundle SHA-256 \texttt{cba512aa\ldots}), with its author's note on which families it contains as special cases.

\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.

\section{The second test: design and registration}\label{sec:design}
\paragraph{The first test.} The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before R3 was implemented. The original claim failed because neither uniform-audit baseline met its criterion against the sealed family; an amended claim, written after development, passed. Appendix~\ref{sec:eval1} gives the verdict, the erratum, and the failure modes it found.

\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.

\paragraph{Lock and draw.} The protocol file binds the code commit, the rules, families, tuned files, manifest, decision rules, predictions, budget, and calibration multipliers. It was signed and tagged \texttt{protocol-v2}, stamped with OpenTimestamps, and pushed with its upgraded proof when the Bitcoin tip was at block \const{974379}. The earliest attestation of the proof is at block $A = \const{974307}$; the draw block is $H = A + \const{144} = \const{974451}$, read once both explorers reported at least six blocks above it with the same hash. Had the push come at or after $H$, the draw would have been kept and its results labelled nonconfirmatory. We assume that the chain both explorers served at that read is the accepted chain and that no miner chose the block at $H$ to influence this evaluation; a later reorganisation deeper than six blocks is not handled and would be reported.

\paragraph{Fresh markets.} The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs, capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix~\ref{app:params}). Six instances and 200 evaluation seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in general.

\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap (\const{9999} resamples) that resamples seeds within each market, pairing the rules' outcomes on each seed; this measures uncertainty over seeds in the six fixed markets, not over new markets or repeated searches. The gain is $g = (\pi^{A} - \pi^{T})/(S^*/N)$, attacked profit minus the profit of the rule's frozen truthful reference, normalised by the first-best surplus per provider; with $N = \const{10}$ providers, the Q1 margin of $\const{0.05}$ is half a percent of the first-best surplus. \emph{Calibration.} A multiplier $\kappa$ replaces each nominal tail $\alpha_e$ by $\kappa\alpha_e$, so $\kappa < \const{1}$ widens the intervals. It was selected from $\{1, 1/2, 1/4\}$ on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references on the headline market and dev0 (pools of \const{20000} seeds per market; Q1 in six equal strata), using \const{10000} selection replications, and admitted on \const{20000} independent validation replications if every endpoint's Clopper--Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confidence $1 - \const{0.01}/m$ with $m = \const{8}$ endpoints for Q1 and $m = \const{36}$ for Q4 (the two-market branch evaluates \const{24} Q4 endpoints and keeps the denominator \const{36}, which is conservative), was at most its allocated tail. The fixtures hold the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or reference selection, and it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q1's categories follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4's market test is an intersection-union test~\cite{berger1996bioequivalence}; the calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods~\cite{morris2019simulation}. Conditional on valid calibrated component $p$-values, Holm controls the Q1 direction family and the six Q4 joint gain-and-efficiency decisions separately; fixture admission does not establish that validity on the fresh-market distributions. Q1's reported intervals use calibrated Bonferroni tails, and its efficiency condition uses a separate nominal one-sided \const{97.5} percent bound. The report stores the calibrated two-sided Q1 value that enters Holm and, for Q4, the raw one-sided bootstrap $p$-values of the two components and the calibrated market value $\max(p'_{\mathrm{gain}}, p'_{\mathrm{eff}})$ with $p' = \min(\const{1}, p/\kappa)$; Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4} print these with their Holm-adjusted values, and an audit script recomputes these values from the records and reproduces each Q1 category and direction rejection and each Q4 decision.

\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{\const{0.78}\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
Question & Rule \\\midrule
Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
Q4 & Per market, an intersection-union test of $g_{F_{000}} - g_{F_{111}} > \const{0.10}$ under D2+H3 and $F_{111}$'s gross efficiency $> \const{0.90}$; the market's $p$-value is the larger of the two calibrated component $p$-values; Holm over the six markets; reported as $k$ of six, with failing markets named and undecided markets marked ``generalisation not established''. \\
"""The churn block of the post-hoc analysis (docs/revision/churn/churn-plan.md, draft 6, sections 2, 4, 5 and 6).

g_p(r, m) = mean over the evaluation seeds of the normalised gain of the D2+H3 winner selected under churn p, against
the rule's frozen truthful reference played under the same churn; p = 0 is the registered no-churn record on the same
seeds. Rules: R3 = F111, C*, B3 = F000. Contrasts d = g(C*) - g(R3), q = g(B3) - g(R3), and their changes from no churn,
paired by seed (base with base, doubled with doubled). Intervals: paired seed bootstrap, 9,999 resamples, percentile 95%;
one resampled index vector per market and replicate, shared by every rule, rate, version and the no-churn records;
pooled = equal-weight mean of the six market means, seeds resampled within each market. The intervals condition on the
markets and the selected policies. Exploratory follow-up: no test, no multiplicity family, no registered decision changes.
"""
